// Verification by detection vector.
//
// After excision, deliver a reporter vector that fluoresces
// only in the presence of the target viral DNA signature.
// No fluorescence = signature eliminated. Fluorescence = residual copies.
//
// This converts "outcome distribution unknown" into a measured fact.
//
// Biology:
//   1. Excise the target (standard CRISPR/meganuclease)
//   2. Wait for clearance (days to weeks)
//   3. Deliver a detection vector carrying:
//      - A CRISPR guide matching the SAME target signature
//      - A deactivated Cas (dCas) fused to a transcriptional activator
//      - A reporter gene (e.g., GFP) under a minimal promoter
//   4. If target DNA remains: dCas binds → activator drives GFP → fluorescence
//      If target DNA is gone: dCas has nothing to bind → no GFP → dark
//   5. Quantify: fraction of cells fluorescent = fraction with residual target
//
// This is not hypothetical. dCas9-VPR reporter systems exist.
// What's new is using it as a formal verification step in the Locus pipeline.
//
// Run with:
//   locus build examples/verification_assay.locus \
//     --genome fixtures/corpus/corpus_genome.fa \
//     --facts examples/verification_assay_facts.json
//
//   locus audit examples/verification_assay.locus \
//     --genome fixtures/corpus/corpus_genome.fa \
//     --facts examples/verification_assay_facts.json

target HSV2Latent {
  organism human
  tolerance 0
  signatures all [ GATCTTGGACGT, TTACGCAAGCTT ]
  in only [ neuron.sensory.sacral ] @measured(human)
}

excise HSV2Latent
